description:
Two byte-identical branches that are meant to normalise an _indeterminate_ probe status to {{UNKNOWN}} use {{||}} where {{&&}} was intended. Because a status string can never equal both {{UP}} and {{{}DOWN{}}}, the condition is a *tautology* — it is always true — so the branch fires unconditionally and clobbers a legitimate {{DOWN}} (or {{{}UP{}}}) with {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}.
h3. Affected code

*{{src/main/java/com/mindarray/nms/ResponseProcessor.java:634}}* (inside {{{}case PING, PORT, NTP, RADIUS, FTP, URL, DOMAIN, EMAIL, CERTIFICATE ->{}}})

 
{noformat}
if (result != null && result.containsKey(STATUS))
{
event.put(STATUS, result.getString(STATUS));
}
else if (!event.getString(STATUS).equalsIgnoreCase(STATUS_UP) && !event.getString(STATUS).equalsIgnoreCase(STATUS_DOWN))
{
event.put(STATUS, STATUS_UNKNOWN);
}
 
{noformat}
 

*{{src/main/java/com/mindarray/nms/CollectorResponseProcessor.java:191}}* — identical code, same enclosing {{{}case{}}}.
h3. Why the condition is always true

For any value of {{{}event.getString(STATUS){}}}:
||status value||{{!equals(UP)}}||{{!equals(DOWN)}}||{{\|\|}} result||
|{{"UP"}}|false|true|*true*|
|{{"DOWN"}}|true|false|*true*|
|{{"UNKNOWN"}}|true|true|*true*|

The {{else if}} therefore always executes when reached, so {{STATUS}} is unconditionally set to {{UNKNOWN}} whenever the probe result carries no explicit status body.
h2. Steps to reproduce
 # Provision any availability / service-check monitor (PING, PORT, URL, NTP, RADIUS, FTP, DOMAIN, EMAIL, or CERTIFICATE).
 # Cause the probe to fail such that the collector returns an event whose top-level {{status}} is {{DOWN}} but whose {{result}} body is null / carries no {{status}} key (e.g. the probe timed out or errored before producing a body).
 # Observe the object status recorded in {{ObjectStatusCacheStore}} (line 639 / 196) and the availability history.

h2. Expected behaviour

The reported {{DOWN}} is preserved; the object is marked DOWN, availability history records a DOWN interval, and DOWN-triggered alert policies fire.
h2. Actual behaviour

The {{else if}} overwrites {{DOWN}} with {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}. The object is recorded {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}, availability history shows an UNKNOWN gap instead of an outage, and *DOWN-triggered policies never fire for that interval* — a genuine outage produces no alert.
h2. Impact
 * *Missed outage alerts* for the entire availability / service-check plugin family.
 * Availability and SLO reporting shows UNKNOWN gaps instead of downtime, understating outages.
 * Present on both the app-side path ({{{}ResponseProcessor{}}}, Standalone/Primary) and the edge-collector path ({{{}CollectorResponseProcessor{}}}, EdgeCollector) — no deployment topology is unaffected.

h2. Root cause

Wrong boolean operator. The branch guard should read "status is neither UP *nor* DOWN", which is {{{}!isUP && !isDOWN{}}}, not {{{}!isUP || !isDOWN{}}}.
h2. Proposed fix

Change {{||}} to {{&&}} at both sites:
{code:java}
else if (!event.getString(STATUS).equalsIgnoreCase(STATUS_UP) && !event.getString(STATUS).equalsIgnoreCase(STATUS_DOWN)) 
{ 
event.put(STATUS, STATUS_UNKNOWN); 
}{code}
{{}}

The two sites are byte-identical and *must be fixed together* to keep the app and edge-collector paths consistent.

{{}}

 
h2. Acceptance criteria
 * {{ResponseProcessor.java:634}} uses {{{}&&{}}}.
 * {{CollectorResponseProcessor.java:191}} uses {{{}&&{}}}.
 * A \{{ {status:"DOWN", result:null}}} event on a PING/PORT/URL monitor records {{DOWN}} (not {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}) in {{ObjectStatusCacheStore}} and fires the availability DOWN policy.

 * A \{{ {status:"UP", result:null}}} event records {{UP}} (not {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}).

 * An event whose status is genuinely neither UP nor DOWN still normalises to {{{}UNKNOWN{}}}.
 * Regression test added covering the DOWN-preserved and UNKNOWN-normalised cases for both processors.

h2. Test plan
 * Unit/integration test asserting the three status inputs above map to the expected stored status, for both {{ResponseProcessor}} and {{{}CollectorResponseProcessor{}}}.
 * Constants only ({{{}STATUS_UP{}}}/{{{}STATUS_DOWN{}}}/{{{}STATUS_UNKNOWN{}}}) per project conventions — no raw string literals.

h2. Risk

Very low. One-operator change in a self-contained {{{}else if{}}}; behaviour for genuinely indeterminate status is unchanged. The only behavioural change is that a definite UP/DOWN is now preserved instead of being discarded.
